{{Short description|Architectural styles and history of Okaiken}}
{{Use mdy dates|date=April 2026}}

[[File:Toyozawa_Castle.png|thumb|right|300px|Toyozawa Castle, built in 1619, exemplifies the shoin-zukuri style that dominated the early Honda Shogunate period.]]

'''Architecture in Okaiken''' ({{nihongo|岡井建築|Okaiken kenchiku}}) has been characterized by wooden structures elevated slightly off the ground, with tiled or thatched roofs. The tradition developed distinctive features including sliding doors (''fusuma'') and other movable partitions used in place of walls, allowing the internal configuration of spaces to be customized for different occasions. Since the 19th century, Okaiken has incorporated much Western, modern, and post-modern architecture into construction and design, and is today a leader in cutting-edge architectural technology.

The Sanese architectural tradition spans from prehistoric pit dwellings to the revolutionary Metabolist Movement of the 1960s, which produced pioneering capsule architecture. The ''machiya'' townhouse tradition, the ''shoin-zukuri'' residential style, and the castle architecture of the Sengoku period all contributed to a rich vernacular heritage. Contemporary Sanese architecture continues to blend traditional aesthetic principles with technological innovation, particularly in earthquake-resistant design and sustainable construction.

==General characteristics==
{{See also|Sanese traditional housing}}

Traditional Sanese architecture is defined by several nearly universal features derived from the archipelago's distinct geography, climate, and cultural development. The choice of materials predominantly favors wood in various forms—planks, straw, tree bark, and paper—for almost all structures. Unlike Western architecture, stone is generally avoided except for specific uses such as temple podia and pagoda foundations, largely due to the seismic activity that characterizes the [[Harujima]] and [[Yagikoshu]] fault lines.

